# ScreenSeeker scheduled jobs.
#
#   sudo cp deploy/screenseeker.cron /etc/cron.d/screenseeker
#
# /etc/cron.d entries carry a user field (here: screenseeker). Adjust paths to
# your install. Both lines source the env file so SCREENSEEKER_DB_PATH and the
# TMDB key match the running service.
#
# Logs go under /var/lib/screenseeker, not /var/log. That is not a style
# choice: /var/log is root-owned and these jobs run as `screenseeker`, so the
# shell cannot create the file and the redirection fails *before* the command
# runs - a silently dead cron that leaves no log to explain itself. The data
# directory is the one path the service user owns and the systemd unit grants
# write access to.

SHELL=/bin/bash

# Keep the mirror fresh: refresh availability for every stale film, nightly at
# 04:00. --yes skips the confirmation prompt. This is the same work the web
# Refresh button does; running it here means the data is fresh before you look.
0 4 * * * screenseeker set -a; . /etc/screenseeker.env; /opt/screenseeker/.venv/bin/screenseeker refresh --yes >> /var/lib/screenseeker/refresh.log 2>&1

# Snapshot the database every day at 03:30, before the refresh writes to it.
30 3 * * * screenseeker set -a; . /etc/screenseeker.env; /opt/screenseeker/deploy/backup.sh >> /var/lib/screenseeker/backup.log 2>&1

# Neither log rotates. They are small - a few lines a night - but if you leave
# this running for years, add them to logrotate or truncate them occasionally.
